# Median of Two Sorted Arrays

**LeetCode:** [4. Median of Two Sorted Arrays](https://leetcode.com/problems/median-of-two-sorted-arrays/)

## Approach 1: Two Pointers (Merge Without Extra Array)

### Intuition
Since both arrays are already sorted, we can use two pointers to traverse them in sorted order without creating a merged array. We only need to process elements until we reach the middle position.

- Maintain pointers `i` and `j` for the two arrays.
- Select the smaller available element at each step.
- Track the previous and current elements using `left` and `right`.
- If the combined length is even, return their average; otherwise, return the middle element.

### C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {
        int n1 = nums1.size(), n2 = nums2.size();

        if (n1 == 0) {
            int mid = n2 / 2;
            if (n2 % 2 == 0)
                return ((long long)nums2[mid - 1] + nums2[mid]) / 2.0;
            return (double)nums2[mid];
        }

        if (n2 == 0) {
            int mid = n1 / 2;
            if (n1 % 2 == 0)
                return ((long long)nums1[mid - 1] + nums1[mid]) / 2.0;
            return (double)nums1[mid];
        }

        if ((n1 + n2) % 2 == 0) {
            int mid1 = (n1 + n2) / 2;
            int mid2 = mid1 + 1;
            int count = 0, num1 = -1, num2 = -1, i = 0, j = 0;

            while (count < mid2) {
                if (i < n1 && (j >= n2 || nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) {
                    count++;
                    if (count == mid1) num1 = nums1[i];
                    if (count == mid2) num2 = nums1[i];
                    i++;
                } else {
                    count++;
                    if (count == mid1) num1 = nums2[j];
                    if (count == mid2) num2 = nums2[j];
                    j++;
                }
            }

            return ((long long)num1 + num2) / 2.0;
        } else {
            int mid = (n1 + n2) / 2 + 1;
            int count = 0, i = 0, j = 0;

            while (count < mid) {
                if (i < n1 && (j >= n2 || nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) {
                    count++;
                    if (count == mid) return (double)nums1[i];
                    i++;
                } else {
                    count++;
                    if (count == mid) return (double)nums2[j];
                    j++;
                }
            }
        }

        return 0.0;
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n+m)\), because at most half of the combined elements are processed.
- **Auxiliary Space Complexity:** \(O(1)\), because no merged array is created.

